WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.270
Good morning, Hank! It's Wednesday! Good morning, Hank, it's--wait. Hold on. Chilean miner coming to the surface.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:08.500
He's safe.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.880
What am I, made of stone?

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.960
So Hank, all day long I've been crying, of course.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.740
Like everyone who's watching this. And Henry has been looking at me all day with his head tilted to the side

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.380
like, "Do you need a diaper change? Is it opposite day?"

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:24.209
"Do you just want someone to hold you? Because I can't hold you. I'm only two foot four, but I can try to arrange for someone."

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.840
"Do you need to take a nap? Sometimes when I do that, it's just cuz I need to take a nap."

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.719
"Is it night-night time?" And I'm trying to explain to him that no, it's the Chilean miners and the Chilean

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:37.349
president and their families and the reunions, oh!

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:41.009
Hank, I don't know if the president of Chile is good at being the president of Chile.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:46.420
But I know this: he is good at being president of the independent nation of making me cry-le.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:46.920
All right, in other news:

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:52.020
Why am I doing my happy dance? Because I couldn't do it in front of hundreds of nerdfighters at the Bean in Chicago because I'm

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.959
too socially awkward, but I can do it in my basement.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.200
By the way, Hank, I think this is what it looks like when hundreds of nerdfighters do their happy dance at the Bean in Chicago.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.320
Hank, the beckoning of lovely was just crazy beautiful,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.840
with like a thousand people coming together with Amy Krause Rosenthal to welcome everything lovely into the world.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:12.720
Also, I got to meet a lot of nerdfighters, several of whom have etched permanent reminders not to forget to be awesome onto their bodies.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.080
And! And! And! And! One time I came up with this idea that romantic relationships are pretty predictable, and that each

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:23.189
romantic relationship has a limited set of possible outcomes: breakup, divorce, or death and that therefore

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.700
a mathematical formula predicting who will end a relationship and when ought to be possible,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.160
if you know some basic facts about the two people involved in the relationship.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.780
And then, lacking the mathematical talent to create such a formula,

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:39.780
I wrote a novel about someone who could write a formula like that, and had the actual formula written by my friend, Brotherhood 2.0

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:47.309
Resident Mathematician and future State Representative Daniel Biss. And then, Hank, someone permanently tattooed that formula under her arm. Is this real-life?

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.300
Hank, in other news of lovely, lots of people have been asking us why we haven't made an "It Gets Better" video.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:58.960
The reason for this is that I feel like other people have said what I would say better than I would say it, so instead of making an "It Gets Better" video,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.800
I have made a playlist called, "It doesn't get better than these 'It Gets Better' videos."

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.659
You can find that playlist in the doobly-doo. There have been some awesome videos,

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:10.799
and you should check it out even if you aren't wondering if it gets better, which it does.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.069
I mean, we live in this irreparable broken world, and I don't wish to deny reality,

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:16.829
but the amazing thing to me is not that we refuse to relinquish

00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:20.780
hope as a species, the amazing thing is that we're right to hold on to hope.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.360
The world may be broken, but hope is not crazy.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.280
For the first 17 days after the mine collapse, those miners lived 2,000 feet under the ground on two days worth of rations

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.920
with no knowledge that anyone was ever going to come for them.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.940
Obviously not all stories end happily, we don't always have good fortune,

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:41.880
but hope gives us as a species, and as individuals, what we otherwise wouldn't have: a chance.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.760
All right Hank, they're about to bring out another guy, so I'm gonna go. I'll see you on Friday.

